window.docContents[197].resolve({"/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--ISO-80000-5-___-Thermodynamics--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-ISO-80000-5":{"contents":"\n\nA catalogued kind pairs a dimensioned quantity-kind with its\nexact source in the series — the part, the printed item designation (e.g. \"5-20.3\"), the\nprincipal quantity symbol, and the coherent SI unit symbol. The citation travels with\nthe kind as data, so the source of each definition can be rendered or audited downstream\nrather than kept in a comment.\n\nRealized as structure CataloguedKind over a DimensionedKind, with cite rendering\ne.g. ISO 80000-5, Second edition, 2019-08 item 5-18. The catalogue lists all 54\nitems in item order — thermodynamic temperature (5-1), heat (5-6.1), entropy (5-18),\nthe thermodynamic potentials (5-20.1 … 5-20.5), the dew-point temperature (5-36), … —\neach paired with its coherent SI unit symbol.\n\nEntropy is M·L²·T⁻²·Θ⁻¹ (item 5-18). The catalogued entropy kind carries\ntemperature-exponent minus one in PhysLib's dimension group — the standard's\ndimensional statement reproduced as a checked computation over the temperature\ngenerator Θ, not an annotation. Uses the dimension map.\n\nProved as entropy_dim_temperature : entropy.dim.temperature = -1 in the Iso80000\nlibrary, discharged by norm_num over the PhysLib dimension lemmas for the temperature\ngenerator. The companions heatCapacity_dim_temperature = -1, heat_dim_length = 2\n(item 5-6.1), and linearExpansionCoefficient_dim_temperature = -1 (item 5-3.1) record\nthe rest of the thermodynamic algebra.\n\nA kelvin and a joule are not commensurable. Thermodynamic temperature and energy are\ndistinct kinds, so their units cannot be compared by ratio — a type-level fact, not a\nruntime check. Uses metrological units.\n\nProved as kelvin_joule_not_commensurable : ¬ kelvin.Commensurable joule by unfolding\nCommensurable to the underlying kinds and decide. The kelvin and the joule are each\nWellFormed (thermodynamic temperature and energy are ratio-scale).\n\n","context":"PropertyKindCalculus Blueprint\u0009ISO 80000-5 — Thermodynamics","header":"16.1. Quantity-kinds and units of ISO 80000-5","id":"/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--ISO-80000-5-___-Thermodynamics--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-ISO-80000-5"},"/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--Units-and-the-Dimension-1-Problem":{"contents":"This chapter states the layer that originally motivated PropertyKindCalculus. Its\nmotivating capstone — the dimension-1 disambiguation — is now proved (see the\nDimension as a Forgetful Functor chapter for the forgetful map it rests on);\nthe unit-arithmetic nodes around it remain planned, carrying\ninformal statements and proof sketches that appear as in-progress goals in the\ndependency graph until a (lean := …) declaration or checked code block is\nattached.\n\nPhysLib's Dimension (a ℚ-exponent free commutative group over the SI base\nquantities) makes every dimension-one quantity equal. Volumetric water content\n(), gravimetric water content (), relative\npermittivity, reflectivity, and emissivity are all dimension one — so a\ndimension-only type system cannot tell them apart. In soil-moisture retrieval\nthat is precisely the class of confusion that silently corrupts a result. The\nkind layer is what restores the distinction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PropertyKindCalculus Blueprint","header":"7. Units and the Dimension-1 Problem","id":"/#PropertyKindCalculus-Blueprint--Units-and-the-Dimension-1-Problem"}});